## What to produce

Write your review to **`/workspace/submission/review.md`**. That is the only
required output, and it must be a real review — at least {{ min_review_chars }}
characters of substance.

Anything else you find useful is yours to decide: structured findings, a
per-section pass, notes on what you could not check. Extra files in
`/workspace/submission/` are archived with the review.

## What the review should cover

Write the review you would send to an editor. There is no template to fill in,
but a useful review of a manuscript generally says:

- what the paper claims, and whether the argument supports it;
- where it does not — a gap in a proof, a claim beyond what is established, a
  quantity asserted without derivation, an attribution that does not hold;
- what is genuinely good about it, since an editor needs that too;
- what you could not verify, and why.

Be specific. A finding that names a location and says what is wrong there is
worth more than a general impression — quote or point to the passage you mean.
Do not pad the review with objections you cannot support; a short review that is
right beats a long one that hedges.

If you are uncertain whether something is a defect, say so and say why, rather
than either suppressing it or asserting it.

## Ground rules

- `/workspace/paper/` is read-only input. Do not modify it.
- Review the manuscript as submitted. Do not rewrite it.
- Do not fabricate citations, quotations, or results.
- Network access depends on how this task was launched. Check rather than
  assume: if you cannot reach the network, assess the manuscript on what is on
  the page, and where a claim would need checking against outside literature,
  say that it needs checking rather than guessing at the answer.

